## 5. Source Code Modification Links

All GF(2¹⁶) functions are extracted from `src/encoding/gf.rs` via
the Aeneas toolchain. The extraction configuration is defined in
[`aeneas-config.yml`](../../aeneas-config.yml).

### Extraction Configuration (`aeneas-config.yml`)

This YAML file (304 lines) drives the entire Rust → Lean 4 extraction
pipeline. It is necessary because the Aeneas/Charon toolchain cannot
extract *all* Rust code (e.g. platform-specific SIMD intrinsics, log
macros, certain prost-generated code), and several extracted definitions
need post-hoc patching to type-check in Lean. The configuration
contains the following sections:

| Section | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| **`aeneas`** | Pins the Aeneas commit hash (`1180be60`) and repository URL, ensuring reproducible extraction. |
| **`upstream`** | Records the upstream Signal `SparsePostQuantumRatchet` repository and the exact commit (`f2589fef`) being verified, linking proofs to a specific source revision. |
| **`crate`** | Specifies the Rust crate directory and package name (`spqr`). |
| **`charon.start_from`** | Enumerates the top-level Rust modules to extract: `util`, `encoding`, `serialize`, `incremental_mlkem768`, `proto`, `kdf`, `chain`, `authenticator`, `v1`. This scopes extraction to the library's public API. |
| **`charon.exclude`** | Lists items that Charon must *skip entirely* because they use unsupported Rust features. For GF(2¹⁶) this excludes `accelerated` (SIMD intrinsics) and `check_accelerated` (the `cpufeatures` macro). Also excludes `log`, certain prost-generated `Debug`/`Hash`/`PartialEq`/`Eq`/`StructuralPartialEq` impls, and a libcrux compat function. |
| **`charon.opaque`** | Declares items that are *extracted as opaque axioms* (type signature only, no body). For GF(2¹⁶) this includes `mul2_u16` and `MulAssign<&GF16>` — both dispatch to SIMD code at runtime — plus `kdf::hkdf_to_slice`, `incremental_mlkem768::encaps2`, and several prost `Message` impls. |
| **`aeneas_args`** | Controls Lean output: `split-files` generates separate `Types.lean` and `Funs.lean` files under `Spqr/Code/`. |
| **`tweaks`** | Post-extraction find/replace substitutions applied to the generated Lean files. These are critical for GF(2¹⁶) verification because they: **(1)** inject a concrete `MulAssign<&GF16>::mul_assign` body that delegates to `unaccelerated::mul` (replacing the opaque axiom), so proofs can reason about multiplication; **(2)** inject a concrete `mul2_u16` body that delegates to `unaccelerated::mul2` (replacing the opaque axiom), so proofs can reason about double-products; **(3)** fix `parallel_mult_loop.body` to properly bind both lanes of the `mul2_u16` result (the original extraction discarded the second component as `_`). Other tweaks handle unrelated modules (prost `PartialOrd`/`Ord` instances, iterator `collect`/`take`, polynomial loop bodies, etc.) and normalize source paths for reproducibility. |

**Why this configuration is needed.** The Rust source uses
`#[cfg(target_arch)]`-gated SIMD intrinsics (x86 `PCLMULQDQ` /
aarch64 `PMULL`) as the default multiplication path. Aeneas cannot
model these intrinsics, so the configuration (a) excludes the
`accelerated` module entirely, (b) marks the dispatch functions
(`mul2_u16`, `MulAssign<&GF16>`) as opaque, and (c) uses post-
extraction tweaks to replace the opaque axioms with concrete
definitions that delegate to the `unaccelerated` software fallback.
This ensures the Lean model is complete and all GF(2¹⁶) functions
have executable definitions that can be verified.

The source modifications applied before extraction are documented in
[`src-modifications.diff`](../../src-modifications.diff).

The mathematical foundation for all proofs (ring homomorphism `φ`,
polynomial `POLY_GF2`, `natToGF2Poly`, `GF216`, etc.) is defined in
[`Spqr/Math/Gf.lean`](../../Spqr/Math/Gf.lean).

